nd carbonyl groups form there before they appear in the SAN (Sabatini et al. 2023)."}},{"id":"butadiene-unit","label":"One butadiene unit (the rubber)","kind":"monomer","atoms":[28,29,30,31,64,65,66,67,68,69,70],"note":{"simple":"Four carbons with a double bond in the middle. Long chains of these make the soft rubber blobs that stop ABS shattering.","deeper":"A cis-1,4 polybutadiene unit, -CH2-CH=CH-CH2-. The SAN branch above grows off this rubber chain (grafting), which glues the rubber blobs into the glassy matrix."}},{"id":"nitrile","label":"Nitrile group (from acrylonitrile)","kind":"functional-group","atoms":[15,16],"note":{"simple":"Nitriles on neighbouring chains pull on each other, making ABS stronger than plain polystyrene.","deeper":"Polar C-N triple bonds; acrylonitrile adds chemical resistance, hardness and a higher heat deflection temperature."}},{"id":"benzene-ring","label":"Benzene ring (from styrene)","kind":"functional-group","atoms":[7,8,9,10,11,12,43,44,45,46,47],"note":{"simple":"The bulky ring that makes the glassy part hard and shiny.","deeper":"Phenyl side groups stiffen the SAN chains."}}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Acrylonitrile butadiene styrene","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Acrylonitrile_butadiene_styrene"},{"label":"Sabatini et al. 2023, Polymers 15:3267 (light-stability of ABS in LEGO bricks)","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/polym15153267"},{"label":"PubChem: 1,3-Butadiene (CID 7845)","url":"https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/compound/7845"}]}
